From 7e407f737056c57d07199470a8da61f55e877fb9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: linux-a9 <linux-a9@localhost>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 10:07:08 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 07/43] PCI: apple-h9p: Add S8003 support

The A9 (TSMC) root complex shares T8010's MMIO layout, but iOS 16's
AppleS800xPCIe/AppleS8003PCIe brings it up differently from the T8010
sequence this driver had, so give S8003 its own common and port
bring-up, following the iOS code (docs/pcie/kernelcache-notes.md in
linux-a9):

- common: rc+0x4 = 0x10 | lane-cfg (only 1 and 3 are valid), port 0
  power bit, wait for rc+0x2c bit 0, rc+0x38 = 1,
- NVMe mode (rc+0x14/0x24) before powering a port marked
  apple,nvme-mode,
- port power-up through rc+0x114/0x124/0x100 with the ready bits in
  rc+0x2c (bit 4) and rc+0x12c, then t-refclk-to-perst before PERST#,
- target speed only through the root port's LNKCTL2, clamped to
  LNKCAP2 (rc+0x4020 is a link-state counter control, not a rate),
- MSI control at port+0x124 (1 | log2(vectors) << 4),
- phy-ip programmed per PHY lane, not per port: lane-cfg 1 gives
  port 0 lanes 0-1 and port 1 lane 2,
- link-up from port+0x88 bit 0, LTSSM state from port+0x804.

Left out on purpose: the ADT apcie-phy/common tunables (only in the
live ADT, not passed to Linux yet), CLKREQ# (iOS doesn't wait for it
on j71t, so the pin keeps its peripheral function and the GPIO is now
optional) and enabling refclk gating.

Common changes:

- take each port's speed from max-link-speed,
- always run the full port sequence: PERST# is asserted when the GPIO
  is requested, so skipping training would leave the endpoint in reset,
- drop a port that fails instead of failing the host bridge, so ECAM
  never reaches a dead link and NVMe and Wi-Fi don't depend on each
  other, and wait 100 ms after link-up before enumeration,
- NVMMU: the SART (HMB) window is optional, a failed NVMMU only
  disables NVMe, all 128 TCBs are allocated, registers go in iOS order
  (scratch page, TCB base, init, SART), and the init busy bit is
  per SoC (bit 16 on S8003),
- FlatDMA TCBs carry one direction (0x200 device writes memory, 0x100
  device reads it), as iOS rejects both; the page count is written
  last, and retiring a slot clears it before invalidating the tag.
  apple_ans_nvmmu_map() gains a direction argument for this.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <maintainer@example.invalid>
